--TEST--
A visibility keyword on a parameter promotes it to a property only in the `constructor`, and is refused on every other method — `rule:classes/delegation-by-field`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `nvs_syntax::ast::Param::is_promoted` is the one home of which parameters
// promote, and `nvs_types::signatures::record_promoted_properties` only ever
// asks it of a constructor — so until `E0722` the keyword written on any other
// method declared nothing, took no slot and was silently ignored, where PHP
// refuses it outright.
//
// All three keywords, on an instance method, a static one and an interface
// declaration alike, sorted in source order: a position that stops refusing
// shows up as a missing line rather than as a parameter that quietly is not a
// property.
interface Sink {
    public function accept(public int $n): void;
}

final class Box {
    public int $kept;

    // The one position that does promote, and the reason the others cannot:
    // a property is a slot on an instance, armed once where the instance is
    // made, and a method called any number of times has no such moment.
    public function constructor(private int $seed) {
        $this->kept = $seed;
    }

    public function set(private int $n): void {
        echo $n, "\n";
    }

    public static function make(protected string $s): string {
        return $s;
    }

    // `inout` and a default are ordinary parameter spellings and say nothing
    // about promotion; the keyword beside them is still the whole mistake.
    public function bump(public inout int $n, private string $tag = "t"): void {
        $n = $n + 1;
        echo $tag, "\n";
    }
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0722]: `public` on a parameter promotes it to a property, and only a `constructor` declares one
   --> %s:13:28
   |
13 |     public function accept(public int $n): void;
   |                            ^^^^^^^^^^^^^ declared `public` on `accept`
   = help: drop the keyword — a parameter is a binding here. To declare a property, write it on the class or promote it in the `constructor`

